{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R57 — a frame whose root is neither EXECUTE nor EXECUTE_RESPONSE is answered `400 invalid_request`.",
    "",
    "⛔ RE-AUTHORED 2026-09-15 (b) against 0.8.2.25, and THE OBLIGATION INVERTED — see F75. This is the",
    "first file through ADR-0003's requirement/item split: the arms, preconditions and surface that used",
    "to live here are now a suite-authored ITEM, and this file states the obligation and stops.",
    "",
    "WHAT INVERTED. Under .21 this row read `the connection MUST be closed`, and this file's own reading",
    "argued — correctly, against that text — that `A CODED FRAME BEFORE THE CLOSE IS NOT FORBIDDEN, AND A",
    "SUITE MUST NOT FAIL IT. The row states one obligation — close.` Under .25 the close is THE PEER'S",
    "CHOICE and the coded frame is the obligation. `close` and `coded frame` swapped places.",
    "",
    "⛔ THE .21 RESULT IS VOID, NOT STALE. 34 of 37 peers were scored FAIL for keeping the connection",
    "open. That is a verdict on a rule that no longer exists. It MUST NOT be diffed against any .25 run",
    "and MUST NOT be cited as evidence about those peers. Those 34 findings were HELD, UNROUTED, pending",
    "CQ-32 — which is the only reason the damage is zero.",
    "",
    "Batch 2 (`connectivity`). Read range: §3.3 whole, §4.11 whole, §6.5, §9.1's row.",
    "NO reference check cites this row — it was reached by reading §3.3 to its end (AP-2)."
  ],
  "id": "ECP-R57",
  "id_status": "allocated",
  "title": "A frame whose envelope root is neither EXECUTE nor EXECUTE_RESPONSE is refused with a coded 400 invalid_request response",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §3.3 (final paragraph)",
  "spec_also": [
    "§4.11 (Pre-admission refusals — the NORMATIVE HOME of the frame obligation, 0.8.2.25)",
    "§4.11's cause/code table (root entity is neither EXECUTE nor EXECUTE_RESPONSE -> 400 invalid_request)",
    "§6.5 (dispatch chain)",
    "§3.1 (the envelope; `root` determines behaviour)",
    "§4.9(c) (deliver-or-signal — scoped to ADMITTED requests, so it does NOT reach this class)",
    "§9.1 ('Pre-admission refusal emission' — the floor row this id binds, REPLACED at 0.8.2.25)"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.25",
  "level": "MUST",
  "level_basis": "keyword",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F49, F75",
  "ruled": "CQ-32 substantially answered by 0.8.2.25 and by NEITHER horn — see the reading",
  "reading": [
    "§3.3, closing sentence at 0.8.2.25: \"These are the only two wire message types. **A frame whose root",
    "entity is neither EXECUTE nor EXECUTE_RESPONSE is invalid, and the peer MUST answer `400",
    "invalid_request` before closing [MUST] (0.8.2.25).** This is a **pre-admission refusal** and **§4.11",
    "is its normative home**; the close itself remains the peer's choice.\"",
    "",
    "§4.11 states the class obligation once: \"A peer that refuses a frame pre-admission MUST put a coded",
    "EXECUTE_RESPONSE on the wire [MUST] — correlated by `request_id` where the id is available, and",
    "otherwise as a best-effort coded frame carrying no correlation. **Whether the peer closes the",
    "connection afterwards is its own choice.**\"",
    "",
    "AND IT NAMES TWO DISTINCT FAILURES, WHICH IS THE PART A SUITE MUST NOT COLLAPSE. §4.11: \"Two",
    "behaviours are non-conformant, and they are distinct failures rather than one\" — dropping the frame",
    "(\"the caller learns nothing until its own §6.11(c) deadline expires, and the refusal is unobservable",
    "to every instrument\") and closing with no coded frame (\"indistinguishable from a network fault",
    "(§4.6), so the caller cannot tell a defect in its own request from a defect in the path — and the two",
    "remedies are opposite\"). ⇒ THEY ARE SCORED SEPARATELY. Collapsing them lets a peer get credit for",
    "the wrong fix, and arch said so in terms when ruling CQ-34/CQ-35.",
    "",
    "THE CODE IS PINNED BY CAUSE, NOT BY CLASS. §4.11: \"The frame obligation belongs to the class; the",
    "CODE belongs to the cause [MUST]. A single code for the class would answer an honest caller under the",
    "wrong reason and send them to the wrong layer.\" This row's cause is the fifth table entry — \"Root",
    "entity is neither EXECUTE nor EXECUTE_RESPONSE\" -> \"400 invalid_request\", stated at \"§3.3, this",
    "section\". ⛔ So `400 hash_mismatch` is non-conformant here: nothing was hashed. So is",
    "`400 non_canonical_ecf`: §4.11 declares it non-conformant on the ADJACENT framing arm because ECF",
    "§5.4 scopes it to tag-policy violations, and this frame is well-formed canonical CBOR carrying no",
    "tag — the code would be wrong for a stronger reason here than there.",
    "",
    "WHAT \"A MESSAGE\" IS, SO THE PROBE IS UNAMBIGUOUS — UNCHANGED FROM .21 AND STILL CORRECT. The row is",
    "about a MESSAGE of the wrong type, not about bytes that fail to decode. A frame that is not CBOR, or",
    "CBOR that is not an envelope, is §4.11's FRAMING arm — a different table row with the same code and a",
    "different cause, and a probe that conflates them cannot attribute its result. So the subject is a",
    "WELL-FORMED envelope, correctly framed (§1.6), whose `root` is a well-formed entity of a type that is",
    "neither `system/protocol/execute` nor `system/protocol/execute/response`.",
    "",
    "⭐ THE CLOSE IS NO LONGER OBSERVABLE EVIDENCE OF ANYTHING, AND THIS IS THE INVERSION IN ONE",
    "SENTENCE. Under .21 the close WAS the obligation and the frame was optional; a suite watched the",
    "socket. Under .25 the frame IS the obligation and the close is optional; a suite reads the response.",
    "A peer that closes after emitting the coded frame is conformant; a peer that closes without one is",
    "not; a peer that keeps the connection open after emitting the coded frame is ALSO conformant. ⇒ The",
    "follow-up-request probe that .21's arms were built around no longer decides this row at all.",
    "",
    "⛔ CQ-32 IS SUBSTANTIALLY ANSWERED AND BY NEITHER HORN — RE-VERIFY BEFORE CLOSING IT. We asked arch",
    "whether §3.3's close-on-unknown governs or ecosystem ADR-0002's MUST-ignore-unknowns does. 0.8.2.25",
    "answers NEITHER: the frame is refused, with a code, and the close is discretionary. So a peer that",
    "IGNORES is non-conformant (no coded frame) and a peer that CLOSES BARE is non-conformant (no coded",
    "frame) — both of the shapes the cohort actually exhibits. ⚠ NOT CLOSED HERE: CQ-32's wording is about",
    "unknown MESSAGE TYPES and ADR-0002's rule is about unknown FIELDS and OPCODES. Those surfaces are",
    "adjacent and not identical, and this file does not get to close a question it routed on a reading of",
    "a different section. The ask stands until arch says so.",
    "",
    "⚠ WHAT NOBODY HAS MEASURED. Under .21, 34 of 37 peers kept serving and 3 closed. Under .25 the",
    "question is whether a CODED FRAME went out, and the .21 probe never captured `status` and `code` on",
    "this surface because the old rule made them irrelevant. ⇒ It is entirely possible that all 37 fail",
    "this row, and entirely possible some already pass it. D15: that is an open measurement, not a",
    "prediction, and this file records no prediction because none is grounded.",
    "",
    "STATE. The row carries no state qualifier. The subject is sent on an ESTABLISHED connection, because",
    "on a pre-handshake connection §4.6's handshake errors could plausibly be read as governing first, and",
    "that reading is not this row's to settle."
  ],
  "conformant": [
    {
      "outcome": "coded_response",
      "status": 400,
      "code": "invalid_request",
      "why": "§4.11's cause/code table, fifth row, stated at §3.3. Whether the peer closes afterwards is its own choice and neither shape is preferred."
    }
  ],
  "non_conformant": [
    {
      "outcome": "silent_drop",
      "why": "§4.11's first named failure. No response and no close: the caller learns nothing until its own §6.11(c) deadline. SCORED SEPARATELY from the bare close — §4.11 calls them distinct failures."
    },
    {
      "outcome": "bare_close",
      "why": "§4.11's second named failure. Indistinguishable from a network fault (§4.6); on a multiplexed connection it destroys unrelated ADMITTED requests, which §4.9(c) forbids for each of them. SCORED SEPARATELY from the silent drop."
    },
    {
      "outcome": "request_admitted",
      "why": "the frame was processed rather than refused. §3.3: it is invalid."
    },
    {
      "outcome": "coded_response_wrong_code",
      "why": "§4.11: the code belongs to the CAUSE. A refusal carrying `hash_mismatch` (nothing was hashed) or `non_canonical_ecf` (ECF §5.4 scopes it to tag-policy violations; this frame is canonical and carries no tag) sends an honest caller to the wrong layer."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
